---
import NoteLayout from '../../layouts/NoteLayout.astro';
import Tex from '../../components/Tex.astro';
---
<NoteLayout slug="adjoint-method"
  tldr="If a simulation depends on many parameters and you only need the gradient of one number, solve one extra linear system with the transposed Jacobian (or run time backward once), and you get the derivative with respect to every parameter at once."
  source="The equilibrium case is the one I needed in my SIGGRAPH Asia 2023 paper with Danyong Zhao and Jernej Barbič, where we fit nonlinear materials to poking measurements by differentiating a quasi-static FEM simulation (Sections 4.4 and 7, Equation 16, Appendix E). The section on poking below walks through it.">

  <h2 id="problem">The problem</h2>
  <p>
    A simulation turns parameters <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{p}\in\mathbb{R}^N`} /> (stiffnesses, control forces, an initial shape)
    into a state <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{x}(\mathbf{p})`} /> (positions, velocities), and we judge the result with a single
    number, a loss <Tex tex={String.raw`L(\mathbf{x}(\mathbf{p}),\mathbf{p})`} />. To optimize, we want the gradient
    <Tex tex={String.raw`dL/d\mathbf{p}`} />: <Tex tex={String.raw`N`} /> numbers. The chain rule says
  </p>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`\frac{dL}{d\mathbf{p}} = \frac{\partial L}{\partial \mathbf{p}} + \underbrace{\frac{\partial L}{\partial \mathbf{x}}}_{1\times n}\;\underbrace{\frac{d\mathbf{x}}{d\mathbf{p}}}_{n\times N}.`} />
  <p>
    The trouble is the middle factor. <Tex tex={String.raw`d\mathbf{x}/d\mathbf{p}`} /> is an <Tex tex={String.raw`n\times N`} /> matrix, one
    column per parameter, and every column costs as much as a simulation. With a thousand parameters, that is a thousand
    simulations for one gradient, which is what finite differences cost too. The adjoint method gets all
    <Tex tex={String.raw`N`} /> numbers for the price of roughly one extra simulation.
  </p>

  <h2 id="static">The idea, for an equilibrium</h2>
  <p>
    Start with a static problem: the state is defined implicitly by an equation <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{g}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{p})=\mathbf{0}`} />,
    for example "internal forces balance external forces" in an elastic solid. Differentiating it gives
  </p>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`\frac{\partial \mathbf{g}}{\partial \mathbf{x}}\frac{d\mathbf{x}}{d\mathbf{p}} + \frac{\partial \mathbf{g}}{\partial \mathbf{p}} = \mathbf{0}
    \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
    \frac{dL}{d\mathbf{p}} = \frac{\partial L}{\partial \mathbf{p}} - \frac{\partial L}{\partial \mathbf{x}}\left(\frac{\partial \mathbf{g}}{\partial \mathbf{x}}\right)^{-1}\frac{\partial \mathbf{g}}{\partial \mathbf{p}}.`} />
  <p>
    Everything now hinges on where you put the parentheses in the product on the right. It is a row vector times a
    square inverse times a wide matrix:
  </p>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`\underbrace{\frac{\partial L}{\partial \mathbf{x}}}_{1\times n}\;\underbrace{\left(\frac{\partial \mathbf{g}}{\partial \mathbf{x}}\right)^{-1}}_{n\times n}\;\underbrace{\frac{\partial \mathbf{g}}{\partial \mathbf{p}}}_{n\times N}`} />
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Right to left</strong> (forward sensitivities): first compute
      <Tex tex={String.raw`(\partial\mathbf{g}/\partial\mathbf{x})^{-1}\,\partial\mathbf{g}/\partial\mathbf{p}`} />, which is
      <Tex tex={String.raw`N`} /> linear solves, one per column. Then a cheap row-times-matrix product.</li>
    <li><strong>Left to right</strong> (adjoint): first compute the row vector
      <Tex tex={String.raw`\boldsymbol{\lambda}^{\mathsf T}=\partial L/\partial\mathbf{x}\,(\partial\mathbf{g}/\partial\mathbf{x})^{-1}`} />,
      which is <em>one</em> linear solve. Then a cheap row-times-matrix product.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Same answer, because matrix multiplication is associative; very different cost. Written out, the adjoint method is
    two lines:
  </p>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`\left(\frac{\partial \mathbf{g}}{\partial \mathbf{x}}\right)^{\mathsf T}\boldsymbol{\lambda} = \left(\frac{\partial L}{\partial \mathbf{x}}\right)^{\mathsf T},
    \qquad
    \frac{dL}{d\mathbf{p}} = \frac{\partial L}{\partial \mathbf{p}} - \boldsymbol{\lambda}^{\mathsf T}\frac{\partial \mathbf{g}}{\partial \mathbf{p}}.`} />
  <p>
    The vector <Tex tex={String.raw`\boldsymbol{\lambda}`} /> is the <em>adjoint</em>: it answers "if I could push on the equation
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{g}=\mathbf{0}`} /> a little, how much would the loss change?" In elasticity,
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\partial\mathbf{g}/\partial\mathbf{x}`} /> is the stiffness matrix, which is symmetric, so the adjoint solve
    reuses the very factorization the simulation already computed. The gradient is then almost free.
  </p>

  <h2 id="poking">In practice: fitting a material by poking</h2>
  <p>
    Here is a real instance of the equilibrium case, from <a href="#references">Chen, Zhao and Barbič (2023)</a>. We
    measured nonlinear elastic materials by poking a layer of material with rigid cylinders and recording the contact
    force at each indentation depth (Section 3). The material energy is controlled by a parameter vector
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\boldsymbol{\mu}`} /> (spline values describing how stiffness changes with stretch, plus a volume
    parameter; Sections 6 and 7). For each poking sample <Tex tex={String.raw`i`} />, the cylinder's vertices are prescribed and a
    quasi-static FEM solve finds the free vertex positions <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{u}^F_i`} /> where the elastic forces on them
    vanish (Section 4.4). The simulated total contact force <Tex tex={String.raw`f^S_i`} /> is then compared to the measured one:
  </p>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`\zeta(\boldsymbol{\mu}) = \sum_i \big\|f^S_i\big(\boldsymbol{\mu},\mathbf{u}^F_i(\boldsymbol{\mu})\big) - f^E_i\big\|^2,
    \qquad
    \mathbf{f}^F\big(\boldsymbol{\mu},\mathbf{u}^F_i(\boldsymbol{\mu})\big) = \mathbf{0}.`} />
  <p>
    This is the setup above with <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{u}^F`} />, <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{p}=\boldsymbol{\mu}`} /> and
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{g}=\mathbf{f}^F`} />, the elastic force on the free vertices. Its Jacobian
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\partial\mathbf{g}/\partial\mathbf{x}`} /> is the tangent stiffness matrix <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{K}^{FF}`} />.
    Differentiating the equilibrium gives the paper's Equation 16:
  </p>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`\frac{\partial \mathbf{u}^F}{\partial \boldsymbol{\mu}} = -\left(\mathbf{K}^{FF}\right)^{-1}\frac{\partial \mathbf{f}^F}{\partial \boldsymbol{\mu}}.`} />
  <p>
    That is the right-to-left grouping: forward sensitivities, one solve per material parameter. In that setting it is
    a natural fit, for two reasons. The optimizer is Gauss-Newton (<a href="/notes/gauss-newton">the next note</a>),
    which needs the derivative of every residual, not just the gradient of the total; and the full second derivatives
    in Equation 15 are built from <Tex tex={String.raw`\partial\mathbf{u}^F/\partial\boldsymbol{\mu}`} /> as well. And every solve
    uses <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{K}^{FF}`} />, the matrix that the Newton solver for the equilibrium has just
    factored, so each parameter costs one back-substitution, while <Tex tex={String.raw`\boldsymbol{\mu}`} /> has only one entry per
    spline control point plus one.
  </p>
  <p>
    The adjoint grouping would also work, and it shows the same trade-off from the other side. Each poking sample
    produces a single number, <Tex tex={String.raw`f^S_i`} />, so one adjoint solve per sample gives that sample's whole row of
    derivatives, whatever the number of parameters:
  </p>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`\mathbf{K}^{FF}\boldsymbol{\lambda}_i = \left(\frac{\partial f^S_i}{\partial \mathbf{u}^F}\right)^{\mathsf T},
    \qquad
    \frac{d f^S_i}{d\boldsymbol{\mu}} = \frac{\partial f^S_i}{\partial \boldsymbol{\mu}} - \boldsymbol{\lambda}_i^{\mathsf T}\frac{\partial \mathbf{f}^F}{\partial \boldsymbol{\mu}},`} />
  <p>
    with the same symmetric matrix and the same factorization. Forward costs one back-substitution per parameter,
    adjoint one per output. With a modest number of spline values both are affordable; with a material described by
    thousands of parameters (say, one per element), the adjoint would be the only sensible choice. Either way the
    derivatives ride on the equilibrium solves, so making those fast matters most; in the paper, that came from
    replacing the 3D simulation with an equivalent 2D axisymmetric one, which is orders of magnitude faster
    (Section 4.1).
  </p>

  <h2 id="dynamic">The same idea, for a simulation in time</h2>
  <p>
    A time-stepping simulation is the same problem with a chain of equations, one per step:
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{x}_{t+1} = \mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x}_t,\mathbf{p}_t)`} /> for <Tex tex={String.raw`t=0,\dots,T-1`} />, and a loss on
    the final state <Tex tex={String.raw`L(\mathbf{x}_T)`} />. Putting the parentheses on the left now means sweeping
    <em>backward in time</em>:
  </p>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`\boldsymbol{\lambda}_T = \left(\frac{\partial L}{\partial \mathbf{x}_T}\right)^{\mathsf T},
    \qquad
    \boldsymbol{\lambda}_t = \left(\frac{\partial \mathbf{f}}{\partial \mathbf{x}_t}\right)^{\mathsf T}\boldsymbol{\lambda}_{t+1},
    \qquad
    \frac{dL}{d\mathbf{p}_t} = \boldsymbol{\lambda}_{t+1}^{\mathsf T}\,\frac{\partial \mathbf{f}}{\partial \mathbf{p}_t}.`} />
  <p>
    The adjoint <Tex tex={String.raw`\boldsymbol{\lambda}_t`} /> carries "how much does the final loss care about the state at
    time <Tex tex={String.raw`t`} />?" from the end of the simulation back to the start. One forward run, one backward run, and
    every per-step parameter gets its derivative along the way. Two practical consequences: the backward pass needs
    the Jacobians at every step, so it needs the stored forward trajectory (long simulations store only checkpoints
    and recompute the rest); and the gradient is exact for the <em>discretized</em> simulation, not just an
    approximation of a continuous one.
  </p>

  <h2 id="demo">Try it</h2>
  <p>
    A ball is launched from the left, with gravity and air drag. At every one of <Tex tex={String.raw`T`} /> time steps a small
    control force <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{u}_t\in\mathbb{R}^2`} /> can push it, so there are <Tex tex={String.raw`N=2T`} /> parameters. The
    loss is the squared distance from where the ball ends up to the target, plus a small penalty on the forces.
    <strong>Compute gradient</strong> runs the adjoint sweep: watch it travel backward from the end of the trajectory,
    leaving at each step an arrow for <Tex tex={String.raw`-dL/d\mathbf{u}_t`} />, the direction that force should change.
    <strong>Optimize</strong> repeats gradient steps. Drag the target anywhere.
  </p>
  <figure class="note-fig adj-demo">
    <canvas id="adj-canvas" width="880" height="560" aria-label="Interactive demo: a ball trajectory, a draggable target, and adjoint gradient arrows along the path."></canvas>
    <div class="adj-controls">
      <button type="button" id="adj-grad">Compute gradient</button>
      <button type="button" id="adj-step">One step</button>
      <button type="button" id="adj-opt">Optimize</button>
      <button type="button" id="adj-reset">Reset</button>
      <label>Steps <Tex tex={String.raw`T`} />: <input type="range" id="adj-T" min="10" max="150" step="10" value="60" /> <span id="adj-Tv">60</span></label>
    </div>
    <div class="adj-read" id="adj-read"></div>
    <figcaption>Black: the trajectory. Gray: the current control forces. Red: the negative gradient for each step's force,
      from one backward pass, scaled so the longest arrow has a fixed length. The cost box compares what each way of
      getting that gradient would take.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Two things to notice. First, the arrows near the end of the path are short and the ones near the start are long:
    a push early on has more time to change where the ball lands, and the adjoint discovers this by itself. Second,
    raise <Tex tex={String.raw`T`} />: the number of parameters grows, the finite-difference bill grows with it, and the adjoint
    stays at one forward and one backward pass.
  </p>

  <h2 id="backprop">You have used it already</h2>
  <p>
    Backpropagation is the adjoint method applied to a neural network: the layers are the time steps, the weights are
    the parameters, and the backward pass is the adjoint sweep. Reverse-mode automatic differentiation is the same
    thing done automatically for any program. The rule of thumb carries over: reverse mode (adjoint) costs about one
    extra pass per <em>output</em>, forward mode about one per <em>input</em>. With one loss and many parameters, the
    adjoint wins; with one parameter and many outputs you care about, forward sensitivities are simpler and just as fast.
  </p>

  <h2 id="checklist">A short checklist</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Check against finite differences</strong> on a few random entries before trusting a hand-written
      adjoint (the demo does this for you; the relative error should be tiny).</li>
    <li><strong>Transpose, don't invert.</strong> The adjoint system uses <Tex tex={String.raw`(\partial\mathbf{g}/\partial\mathbf{x})^{\mathsf T}`} />;
      for symmetric stiffness matrices that is the same matrix and the same factorization.</li>
    <li><strong>Mind the memory</strong> in long time-dependent problems: the backward pass needs the forward states.
      Checkpointing trades memory for recomputation.</li>
    <li><strong>Differentiate what you actually run.</strong> Deriving the adjoint of the continuous equations and then
      discretizing it ("optimize, then discretize") gives a gradient that can disagree with your discrete loss;
      differentiating the discrete steps ("discretize, then optimize"), as above, does not.</li>
    <li><strong>Count outputs, not just parameters.</strong> Adjoint costs one solve per output you need derivatives
      of, forward sensitivities one per parameter. For a least-squares fit with a handful of parameters and many
      residuals, forward can be the simpler choice.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="references">References</h2>
  <ul class="refs">
    <li>Huanyu Chen, Danyong Zhao, Jernej Barbič. Capturing Animation-Ready Isotropic Materials Using Systematic Poking.
      <em>ACM Transactions on Graphics</em> 42(6) (SIGGRAPH Asia 2023).
      <a href="https://doi.org/10.1145/3618406">doi:10.1145/3618406</a></li>
  </ul>
</NoteLayout>

<style>
  .adj-demo canvas { width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 6px; touch-action: none; cursor: crosshair; }
  .adj-controls { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .5em 1em; align-items: center; margin-top: .6em; }
  .adj-controls button { font: inherit; padding: .3em .8em; cursor: pointer; background: var(--bg); color: var(--ink); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 4px; }
  .adj-controls button:hover { border-color: var(--link); }
  .adj-controls input { vertical-align: middle; }
  .adj-read { margin-top: .6em; font-size: .9em; line-height: 1.5; }
  .adj-read table { border-collapse: collapse; margin-top: .3em; }
  .adj-read td { padding: .1em .8em .1em 0; }
</style>

<script>
  import { simulate, loss, grad } from '../../scripts/adjoint-core.js';
  const cv = document.getElementById('adj-canvas') as HTMLCanvasElement;
  const ctx = cv.getContext('2d')!;
  const W = cv.width, H = cv.height;
  const X0 = 0, X1 = 11, Y0 = -1.5, Y1 = 5.5; // world window
  const sx = (x: number) => ((x - X0) / (X1 - X0)) * W, sy = (y: number) => H - ((y - Y0) / (Y1 - Y0)) * H;
  const wx = (px: number) => X0 + (px / W) * (X1 - X0), wy = (py: number) => Y0 + ((H - py) / H) * (Y1 - Y0);
  const p0 = [1, 1], v0 = [6, 9];
  let T = 60, u = new Array(2 * T).fill(0), target = [8, 1.5];
  let G: { g: Float64Array } | null = null, reveal = 1, anim = 0, optimizing = false, check = '';
  const css = (v: string) => getComputedStyle(document.documentElement).getPropertyValue(v).trim() || '#000';

  function arrow(x: number, y: number, dx: number, dy: number, col: string, w = 2) {
    const ax = sx(x), ay = sy(y), bx = sx(x + dx), by = sy(y + dy);
    const len = Math.hypot(bx - ax, by - ay); if (len < 1.5) return;
    ctx.strokeStyle = col; ctx.fillStyle = col; ctx.lineWidth = w;
    ctx.beginPath(); ctx.moveTo(ax, ay); ctx.lineTo(bx, by); ctx.stroke();
    const a = Math.atan2(by - ay, bx - ax), h = Math.min(8, len * 0.4);
    ctx.beginPath(); ctx.moveTo(bx, by);
    ctx.lineTo(bx - h * Math.cos(a - 0.45), by - h * Math.sin(a - 0.45));
    ctx.lineTo(bx - h * Math.cos(a + 0.45), by - h * Math.sin(a + 0.45)); ctx.closePath(); ctx.fill();
  }

  function draw() {
    const ink = css('--ink'), muted = css('--muted'), link = css('--link'), line = css('--line'), bg = css('--bg');
    ctx.fillStyle = bg; ctx.fillRect(0, 0, W, H);
    ctx.strokeStyle = line; ctx.lineWidth = 1; ctx.beginPath(); ctx.moveTo(0, sy(0)); ctx.lineTo(W, sy(0)); ctx.stroke();
    const sim = simulate(p0, v0, u, T);
    // control forces
    for (let t = 0; t < T; t++) arrow(sim.P[t][0], sim.P[t][1], u[2 * t] * 0.06, u[2 * t + 1] * 0.06, muted, 1.5);
    // trajectory
    ctx.strokeStyle = ink; ctx.lineWidth = 2.5; ctx.beginPath();
    sim.P.forEach((p, i) => (i ? ctx.lineTo(sx(p[0]), sy(p[1])) : ctx.moveTo(sx(p[0]), sy(p[1])))); ctx.stroke();
    ctx.fillStyle = ink; ctx.beginPath(); ctx.arc(sx(p0[0]), sy(p0[1]), 6, 0, 7); ctx.fill();
    const pT = sim.P[T]; ctx.beginPath(); ctx.arc(sx(pT[0]), sy(pT[1]), 5, 0, 7); ctx.fill();
    // target
    ctx.strokeStyle = link; ctx.lineWidth = 2.5; ctx.beginPath(); ctx.arc(sx(target[0]), sy(target[1]), 11, 0, 7); ctx.stroke();
    ctx.beginPath(); ctx.arc(sx(target[0]), sy(target[1]), 3, 0, 7); ctx.fillStyle = link; ctx.fill();
    ctx.setLineDash([4, 4]); ctx.strokeStyle = link; ctx.lineWidth = 1;
    ctx.beginPath(); ctx.moveTo(sx(pT[0]), sy(pT[1])); ctx.lineTo(sx(target[0]), sy(target[1])); ctx.stroke(); ctx.setLineDash([]);
    // adjoint arrows, revealed from the end backward
    if (G) {
      let m = 1e-12; for (let t = 0; t < T; t++) m = Math.max(m, Math.hypot(G.g[2 * t], G.g[2 * t + 1]));
      const s = 1.1 / m, first = Math.floor(T * (1 - reveal));
      for (let t = T - 1; t >= first; t--) arrow(sim.P[t][0], sim.P[t][1], -G.g[2 * t] * s, -G.g[2 * t + 1] * s, link, 2);
      if (reveal < 1) { const q = sim.P[Math.max(first, 0)]; ctx.fillStyle = link; ctx.beginPath(); ctx.arc(sx(q[0]), sy(q[1]), 8, 0, 7); ctx.fill();
        ctx.font = '600 15px system-ui, sans-serif'; ctx.fillText('λ', sx(q[0]) + 10, sy(q[1]) - 10); }
    }
    readout(sim);
  }

  function readout(sim: ReturnType<typeof simulate>) {
    const N = 2 * T, L = loss(sim, u, target);
    (document.getElementById('adj-read') as HTMLElement).innerHTML =
      `Loss <strong>${L.toFixed(4)}</strong> · parameters <strong>N = ${N}</strong>` +
      `<table><tr><td>Finite differences</td><td>${N + 1} simulations</td></tr>` +
      `<tr><td>Forward sensitivities</td><td>1 simulation carrying ${N} tangent vectors</td></tr>` +
      `<tr><td><strong>Adjoint</strong></td><td><strong>1 simulation + 1 backward pass</strong></td></tr></table>` +
      (check ? `<span class="muted">${check}</span>` : '');
  }

  function fdCheck(sim: ReturnType<typeof simulate>) {
    const g = grad(sim, u, target).g; let worst = 0, gmax = 1e-12;
    for (let i = 0; i < g.length; i++) gmax = Math.max(gmax, Math.abs(g[i]));
    for (let k = 0; k < 4; k++) {
      const i = Math.floor(Math.random() * u.length), h = 1e-6, a = u.slice(), b = u.slice(); a[i] += h; b[i] -= h;
      const fd = (loss(simulate(p0, v0, a, T), a, target) - loss(simulate(p0, v0, b, T), b, target)) / (2 * h);
      worst = Math.max(worst, Math.abs(fd - g[i]) / gmax);
    }
    check = `Check against finite differences on 4 random entries: largest error ${worst.toExponential(1)}, relative to the largest gradient entry.`;
  }

  function computeGrad(animate: boolean) {
    const sim = simulate(p0, v0, u, T); G = grad(sim, u, target); fdCheck(sim);
    cancelAnimationFrame(anim);
    if (!animate) { reveal = 1; draw(); return; }
    const t0 = performance.now();
    const tick = (now: number) => { reveal = Math.min(1, (now - t0) / 1100); draw(); if (reveal < 1) anim = requestAnimationFrame(tick); };
    anim = requestAnimationFrame(tick);
  }

  function step() {
    const sim = simulate(p0, v0, u, T); const g = grad(sim, u, target).g;
    for (let i = 0; i < u.length; i++) u[i] -= 0.3 * g[i] / sim.dt;
    G = null; // arrows are drawn scaled to the largest entry, so they would look large even near the optimum
  }

  function optimize() {
    if (optimizing) { optimizing = false; return; }
    optimizing = true; let k = 0; cancelAnimationFrame(anim);
    const btn = document.getElementById('adj-opt')!; btn.textContent = 'Stop';
    const tick = () => {
      if (!optimizing || k++ > 160) { optimizing = false; btn.textContent = 'Optimize'; fdCheck(simulate(p0, v0, u, T)); draw(); return; }
      step(); draw(); anim = requestAnimationFrame(tick);
    };
    anim = requestAnimationFrame(tick);
  }

  document.getElementById('adj-grad')!.addEventListener('click', () => computeGrad(true));
  document.getElementById('adj-step')!.addEventListener('click', () => { step(); fdCheck(simulate(p0, v0, u, T)); draw(); });
  document.getElementById('adj-opt')!.addEventListener('click', optimize);
  document.getElementById('adj-reset')!.addEventListener('click', () => { optimizing = false; u = new Array(2 * T).fill(0); G = null; check = ''; draw(); });
  const Ts = document.getElementById('adj-T') as HTMLInputElement;
  Ts.addEventListener('input', () => { T = +Ts.value; (document.getElementById('adj-Tv') as HTMLElement).textContent = String(T);
    u = new Array(2 * T).fill(0); G = null; check = ''; draw(); });

  let dragging = false;
  const pos = (e: PointerEvent) => { const r = cv.getBoundingClientRect(); return [wx(((e.clientX - r.left) / r.width) * W), wy(((e.clientY - r.top) / r.height) * H)]; };
  cv.addEventListener('pointerdown', (e) => { dragging = true; cv.setPointerCapture(e.pointerId); target = pos(e); G = null; check = ''; draw(); });
  cv.addEventListener('pointermove', (e) => { if (dragging) { target = pos(e); G = null; draw(); } });
  cv.addEventListener('pointerup', () => { dragging = false; });
  matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').addEventListener('change', draw);
  draw();
</script>
